        <h2 id="dashboard">Dashboard</h2>
        <p>The dashboard refreshes every few seconds and shows:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>the <strong>service state</strong> &mdash; running, stopped or not installed &mdash; with version, process id, uptime and the Windows account backups run as;</li>
            <li>one row per job with its state (<em>Scheduled</em>, <em>Running&hellip;</em>, <em>Disabled</em>, <em>Schedule error</em>), next run, last run and last result, highlighted in red after a failure;</li>
            <li>a red banner with every active <a href="#rpo">RPO alert</a>;</li>
            <li>the reason when the control panel cannot reach the service, so you are never debugging blind.</li>
        </ul>
        <screenshot name="dashboard" caption="All jobs healthy, with their next and last runs." />

        <h2 id="history">History</h2>
        <p>Every database backup attempt is a row: start time, job, database, type actually taken, trigger, result, duration, size and file. Filter by job with the <strong>Job</strong> box and select a row for the full details: SQL Server's own message, <code>RESTORE VERIFYONLY</code> result, retention actions and the off-site upload.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Trigger</strong> column shows why a run happened: <em>Scheduled</em>, <em>CatchUp</em> (a missed run after the service was down), <em>Manual</em> (Run now) or <em>ManualStandalone</em> (run inside the app while the service was stopped).</p>
        <p>Failures carry the real SQL Server error. Permission errors also explain which Windows account was used and how to fix it &mdash; see <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
        <screenshot name="history" caption="History with the details of a verified, off-site-copied full backup." />

        <h2 id="reports">Reports</h2>
        <p>The <strong>Reports</strong> page summarises the last 30 days:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Success rate</strong> and the number of runs and failures (a failed off-site upload counts as a failure here);</li>
            <li><strong>Current full-backup footprint</strong> &mdash; the size of the newest full backup of every database added together;</li>
            <li><strong>Bytes written</strong> in the period;</li>
            <li>per job and database: last full backup and its size, success rate, average and maximum duration, growth over 30 days and a 14-day size chart.</li>
        </ul>
        <screenshot name="reports" caption="Two failures in 964 runs show up as 97% on the affected databases." />

        <h2 id="email">Email alerts</h2>
        <p>In <strong>Settings &rarr; Email notifications</strong>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Tick <strong>Send email reports about backup runs</strong>.</li>
            <li>Enter the <strong>SMTP server</strong> and <strong>Port</strong>. Tick <strong>TLS</strong> for STARTTLS (normally port 587); an internal relay often uses port 25 without TLS. Implicit TLS on port 465 is not supported.</li>
            <li>Enter <strong>Username</strong> and <strong>Password</strong> if the server requires authentication (the password is DPAPI-encrypted), the <strong>From address</strong> and one or more <strong>To address(es)</strong> separated by <code>;</code> or <code>,</code>.</li>
            <li>Keep <strong>Only alert when a backup fails</strong> ticked to avoid daily noise, or untick it to receive a report after every run.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Save settings</strong>, then <strong>Send test email</strong>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The email lists every database with its result, duration, file, size, notes and error.</p>
        <screenshot name="settings-notifications" caption="Email, webhook and Event Log alert settings." />

        <h2 id="webhook">Slack, Microsoft Teams and Discord webhooks</h2>
        <p>Tick <strong>POST results to a webhook</strong>, paste the incoming-webhook URL from Slack, Teams (Workflows) or Discord, and click <strong>Send test</strong>. The JSON payload carries a human-readable <code>text</code> (Slack, Teams) and <code>content</code> (Discord), plus structured data for your own automation:</p>
        <pre><code>{
  "event": "backup-result",
  "text": "✅ SqlBackup job 'Nightly full backup' on SQL01: OK (6 databases)",
  "content": "✅ SqlBackup job 'Nightly full backup' on SQL01: OK (6 databases)",
  "machine": "SQL01",
  "timestampUtc": "2026-10-18T01:31:04+00:00",
  "job": "Nightly full backup",
  "success": true,
  "entries": [
    {
      "database": "SalesDB",
      "type": "Full",
      "success": true,
      "offsiteSuccess": true,
      "file": "D:\\SqlBackups\\SalesDB\\SalesDB_Full_20261018_013000.bak",
      "sizeBytes": 11165696,
      "durationSeconds": 0.7,
      "error": null
    }
  ],
  "details": []
}</code></pre>
        <p><em>Only alert when a backup fails</em> applies to webhooks as well.</p>

        <h2 id="event-log">Windows Event Log</h2>
        <p>Tick <strong>Write results to the Windows Event Log</strong> to log every run to the Application log with source <code>SqlBackup</code> &mdash; ideal for SCOM, Zabbix, PRTG, Datadog or any tool that already watches the Event Log.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th scope="col">Event ID</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>1000</code></td><td>Backup job succeeded</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>1001</code></td><td>Backup job failed (or an off-site copy failed)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>1002</code></td><td>RPO breach: no successful backup within the configured time</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="rpo">Missing-backup (RPO) alerts</h2>
        <p>A schedule-based alert only fires when a job runs and fails. Many real backup outages are quieter: a job was disabled during maintenance and never re-enabled, a cron expression was mistyped, or the service was stopped. <strong>RPO alerts</strong> catch those.</p>
        <p>In the job editor, under <strong>Monitoring</strong>, set <strong>Alert when there is no successful backup for</strong> <em>N</em> hours (0 turns it off). For a daily job, 26 hours gives a two-hour grace period; for a 30-minute log backup, 2 hours is typical.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>The service checks every 15 minutes &mdash; per database for explicit database lists, per job for the discovery modes.</li>
            <li>A breach appears on the dashboard and is sent by email, webhook and Event Log &mdash; even when <em>Only alert when a backup fails</em> is ticked.</li>
            <li>The alert repeats once a day until a successful backup resolves it.</li>
        </ul>

